% ================================================================ bignum
(bignumlibrary.ps) sc_h1
(A bignum is a decimal number with a fixed number of significant digits, set with setbnsize. You make one from an integer, a real or a string with setbn, combine bignums with the procedures below, and every result is rounded to the current precision, with halves going away from zero. Results print as sign, first digit, point, remaining digits, e, exponent: +1.25e-3 means 1.25 x 10^-3 = 0.00125.) sc_para

(Setting the precision and making bignums) sc_h2
(20 setbnsize)
	(Sets the working precision to 20 significant digits, the default. It leaves nothing on the stack; every later result is rounded to this many digits.) sc_row
(509 setbn)
	(Converts an integer to a bignum. Results print as sign, one digit, point, the remaining digits, then e and the power of ten: +5.09e2 means 5.09 x 10^2 = 509.) sc_row
(\(-1.25e-3\) setbn)
	(A string can carry a sign, a decimal point and an exponent. -1.25e-3 is -0.00125.) sc_row
(\(.5\) setbn)
	(A leading decimal point is fine: .5 is 0.5, printed as 5 x 10^-1.) sc_row
(\(1E5\) setbn)
	(The exponent marker can be e or E: 1E5 is 100000.) sc_row
(\(123456789012345678901234567890\) setbn)
	(This 30-digit number doesn't fit in 20 digits, so it is rounded, halves away from zero, and the exponent e29 keeps its size.) sc_row
(3.14159265 setbn)
	(A PostScript real carries only the 6 digits Ghostscript prints for it, so the rest of pi is lost before setbn even sees it.) sc_row
(\(3.14159265\) setbn)
	(The same number written as a string keeps all 9 digits. Use strings whenever precision matters.) sc_row
(\(3.5\) setbn setbn)
	(setbn also accepts an existing bignum and returns a copy rounded to the current precision.) sc_row
(\(12x\) setbn)
	(Deliberate error: 12x is not a number, so setbn raises syntaxerror rather than guessing.) sc_row
(0 setbnsize)
	(Deliberate error: the precision must be a whole number of at least 1, so 0 raises rangecheck and the precision stays at 20.) sc_row

(Converting back) sc_h2
(\(2.5\) setbn bncvs)
	(bncvs turns a bignum into a string, the same text bnPrint shows. It is shown in parentheses because the result is a PostScript string.) sc_row
(\(-1.5e3\) setbn bncvr)
	(bncvr converts back to an ordinary PostScript real, here -1500. A real keeps only about 7 digits and can't go beyond about 3.4e38.) sc_row
(\(1e-3\) setbn bnPrint)
	(bnPrint writes the bignum to the console and leaves nothing on the stack, so there is no result to show here.) sc_row

(Arithmetic) sc_h2
(509 setbn 67 setbn addbn)
	(Addition: 509 + 67 = 576.) sc_row
(\(0.1\) setbn \(0.2\) setbn addbn)
	(Decimal arithmetic is exact: 0.1 + 0.2 is exactly 0.3. Binary floating point, as in most programming languages, gives 0.30000000000000004.) sc_row
(67 setbn 509 setbn subbn)
	(Subtraction takes the second number from the first: 67 - 509 = -442.) sc_row
(1 setbn \(1e-25\) setbn subbn)
	(1 - 10^-25 needs 25 digits to write out exactly. At 20 digits it rounds back to 1.) sc_row
(12 setbn 34 setbn mulbn)
	(Multiplication: 12 x 34 = 408.) sc_row
(\(123456789.123456789\) setbn \(987654321.987654321\) setbn mulbn)
	(The exact product has 36 digits, so it is rounded to 20.) sc_row
(1 setbn 7 setbn divbn)
	(Division: 1/7 = 0.142857142857..., cut to 20 digits with the last one rounded.) sc_row
(22 setbn 7 setbn divbn)
	(22/7, the classic approximation of pi. It is right to only 3 digits; compare pibn further down.) sc_row
(-1 setbn 8 setbn divbn)
	(Signs follow the usual rules: -1/8 = -0.125, which is exact.) sc_row
(1 setbn 0 setbn divbn)
	(Deliberate error: dividing by zero raises undefinedresult, the same error PostScript's own div gives.) sc_row

(Sign and integer parts) sc_h2
(\(3.75\) setbn negbn)
	(negbn flips the sign: 3.75 becomes -3.75.) sc_row
(\(-3.75\) setbn absbn)
	(absbn gives the size without the sign: 3.75.) sc_row
(\(-12.5\) setbn truncbn)
	(truncbn drops the fraction, rounding toward zero: -12.5 becomes -12.) sc_row
(\(-12.5\) setbn roundbn)
	(roundbn rounds to the nearest whole number, with halves going away from zero: -12.5 becomes -13.) sc_row
(\(2.5\) setbn roundbn)
	(Halves go away from zero for positive numbers too: 2.5 becomes 3.) sc_row
(\(0.4\) setbn roundbn)
	(0.4 is nearer to 0 than to 1, so it rounds to 0.) sc_row

(Comparison) sc_h2
(2 setbn 3 setbn cmpbn)
	(cmpbn answers -1, 0 or 1 when the first number is less than, equal to or greater than the second. 2 is less than 3.) sc_row
(3 setbn 2 setbn cmpbn)
	(3 is greater than 2, so 1.) sc_row
(\(123\) setbn \(1.23e2\) setbn cmpbn)
	(Numbers compare by value, not by how they were written: 123 and 1.23e2 are equal.) sc_row
(\(123\) setbn \(1.23e2\) setbn eqbn)
	(eqbn returns true when the two values are equal.) sc_row
(2 setbn 3 setbn nebn)
	(nebn returns true when they differ.) sc_row
(-2 setbn -3 setbn gtbn)
	(gtbn asks whether the first is greater than the second. -2 is greater than -3.) sc_row
(-2 setbn -3 setbn gebn)
	(gebn asks greater than or equal.) sc_row
(\(1e-50\) setbn 0 setbn ltbn)
	(ltbn asks whether the first is less than the second. 10^-50 is tiny but still positive, so the answer is false.) sc_row
(5 setbn 5 setbn lebn)
	(lebn asks less than or equal, so equal values give true.) sc_row
(0 setbn \(-0\) setbn eqbn)
	(Zero has no sign: 0 and -0 are equal.) sc_row
(\(-0.000\) setbn bniszero)
	(bniszero tests for zero however it was written, with a sign or extra zeros.) sc_row
(\(1e-99\) setbn bniszero)
	(10^-99 is very small, but it is not zero.) sc_row

(Functions at 30 digits) sc_h2
(30 setbnsize)
	(Raises the precision to 30 digits for the rows below.) sc_row
(pibn)
	(pi, from Machin's formula: pi = 16 atan\(1/5\) - 4 atan\(1/239\).) sc_row
(1 setbn sinbn)
	(sin of 1 radian, from its Taylor series after the angle is brought into the range -pi to pi.) sc_row
(1 setbn cosbn)
	(cos of 1 radian.) sc_row
(1 setbn tanbn)
	(tan of 1 radian, worked out as sin divided by cos.) sc_row
(\(1e50\) setbn sinbn)
	(sin of 10^50 radians. Reducing so large an angle needs pi to about 86 digits: the 30 shown, 6 guard digits and 50 more for the size of the angle; the library raises its working precision automatically, so every digit shown is correct.) sc_row
(2 setbn lnbn)
	(The natural logarithm of 2.) sc_row
(10 setbn lnbn)
	(ln 10. The constants ln 2 and ln 10 are worked out once per precision and then reused.) sc_row
(2 setbn log10bn)
	(The base-10 logarithm of 2.) sc_row
(1000 setbn log10bn)
	(Exact powers of ten give exact answers: log10 of 1000 is 3.) sc_row
(0 setbn lnbn)
	(Deliberate error: the logarithm of 0 is undefined, so lnbn raises rangecheck.) sc_row
(-1 setbn log10bn)
	(Deliberate error: logarithms of negative numbers are undefined too.) sc_row

(Roots, powers and more functions) sc_h2
(2 setbn sqrtbn)
	(The square root of 2, by Newton's method starting from the ordinary real square root. Each step doubles the number of correct digits.) sc_row
(1 setbn expbn)
	(e, the base of natural logarithms. expbn writes x as k ln 10 + r, so e^x = 10^k e^r, and sums the Taylor series of e^r.) sc_row
(\(-50\) setbn expbn)
	(e^-50 is tiny, but a bignum keeps its full precision: there is no underflow.) sc_row
(2 setbn \(0.5\) setbn powbn)
	(2 to the power 0.5 is the square root of 2 again. A power that is not a whole number is worked out as e^\(y ln x\).) sc_row
(2 setbn 90 setbn powbn)
	(Whole-number powers use repeated squaring instead, so 2^90 is exact: all 28 digits.) sc_row
(-2 setbn 3 setbn powbn)
	(A whole-number power of a negative number is allowed: \(-2\)^3 = -8.) sc_row
(1 setbn atanbn)
	(atan 1 = pi/4. atanbn halves the angle twice before summing its series, so the series converges fast.) sc_row
(\(0.5\) setbn asinbn)
	(asin 0.5 = pi/6.) sc_row
(\(0.5\) setbn acosbn)
	(acos 0.5 = pi/3.) sc_row
(-1 setbn -1 setbn atan2bn)
	(atan2bn takes y, then x, and gives the angle of the point \(x, y\): here -3pi/4, in the correct quadrant, which atan of y/x alone can't tell.) sc_row
(1 setbn sinhbn)
	(The hyperbolic sine, \(e^x - e^-x\)/2. For small x it uses its own series, so no digits are lost to cancellation.) sc_row
(1 setbn tanhbn)
	(The hyperbolic tangent. coshbn completes the set.) sc_row
(25 factbn)
	(25 factorial, exact: 26 digits fit in 30.) sc_row
(60 30 binombn)
	(60 choose 30, the number of ways to pick 30 items from 60: exact.) sc_row
(-1 setbn sqrtbn)
	(Deliberate error: negative numbers have no real square root, so sqrtbn raises rangecheck. Complex.ps has csqrt for that.) sc_row

(The same calculation at different precisions) sc_h2
(5 setbnsize pibn)
	(pi to 5 significant digits: 3.1416, rounded from 3.14159.) sc_row
(20 setbnsize pibn)
	(pi to 20 digits.) sc_row
(50 setbnsize pibn)
	(pi to 50 digits.) sc_row
(100 setbnsize pibn)
	(pi to 100 digits. This takes a few seconds the first time; after that the value is cached.) sc_row
(50 setbnsize 1 setbn 7 setbn divbn)
	(1/7 to 50 digits: the 142857 pattern repeats right up to the final, rounded digit.) sc_row
(50 setbnsize 2 setbn lnbn)
	(ln 2 to 50 digits.) sc_row

(Exact big integers) sc_h2
(70 setbnsize)
	(70 digits is enough to hold the next results exactly.) sc_row
(1 setbn 1 1 50 { setbn mulbn } for)
	(50 factorial, 1 x 2 x 3 x ... x 50. All 65 digits are exact; the trailing zeros pad it to 70 digits.) sc_row
(1 setbn 1 1 200 { pop 2 setbn mulbn } for)
	(2 to the power 200, built by 200 doublings: 61 exact digits.) sc_row
(\(1e30\) setbn 1 setbn addbn)
	(10^30 + 1 needs 31 digits. At 70 digits the final 1 is kept.) sc_row
(20 setbnsize)
	(Back to the default precision of 20 digits.) sc_row

% ================================================================ Trig
(Trig.ps) sc_h1
(CORDIC builds an angle from the fixed steps atan\(1\), atan\(1/2\), atan\(1/4\), and so on, using only additions and halvings. Results are ordinary PostScript reals, good to about 6 significant digits with the default 40 iterations.) sc_para

(Functions) sc_h2
(1 corsin)
	(sin of 1 radian as a PostScript real. Compare 0.841471 with the 30-digit sinbn result above.) sc_row
(4 corcos)
	(cos of 4 radians. An angle of 4 lies in the third quadrant, where cos is negative.) sc_row
(-2 cortan)
	(tan of -2 radians.) sc_row
(1 corcot)
	(Cotangent, 1/tan.) sc_row
(1 corsec)
	(Secant, 1/cos.) sc_row
(1 corcsc)
	(Cosecant, 1/sin.) sc_row
(1 1 coratan)
	(coratan takes x, then y, and returns atan\(y/x\). Here atan\(1\) = pi/4.) sc_row
(1 -1 coratan)
	(With y negative the angle is negative: -pi/4.) sc_row
(-1 1 coratan)
	(atan\(y/x\) depends only on the ratio, so x = -1, y = 1 also gives atan\(-1\) = -pi/4. The result is always between -pi/2 and pi/2.) sc_row
(-1 -1 coratan)
	(Both negative: the ratio is 1, so pi/4.) sc_row
(0 1 coratan)
	(x = 0 is a vertical line: the angle is pi/2 when y is positive.) sc_row
(0 0 coratan)
	(Deliberate error: with x and y both 0 there is no angle, so coratan raises undefinedresult.) sc_row
(0.5 40 cordiccossin)
	(The routine behind corsin and corcos: an angle and an iteration count go in, and cos and sin come out together as a 2x1 matrix, shown as [cos; sin].) sc_row
(1 1 8 cordicatan)
	(The routine behind coratan, with only 8 iterations: 0.778 instead of 0.785, off by about 0.007. More iterations give more correct digits.) sc_row
(1 1 40 cordicatan)
	(With 40 iterations the answer matches pi/4 = 0.785398 to the full precision of a real.) sc_row

% ---- comparison table
/sc_tablerow { % [ (c1) (c2) ... ] font gray
	setgray /sc_tf exch def /sc_cells exch def
	14 sc_need sc_tf 8.5 sc_font
	0 1 sc_cells length 1 sub { /sc_k exch def
		sc_L sc_k sc_W mul sc_cells length div add sc_y 9 sub moveto
		sc_cells sc_k get show
	} for
	12 sc_down
} def
/sc_r2s { 12 string cvs } def
/sc_deg { 180 mul pi div } def

(CORDIC against Ghostscript's built-in sin and cos) sc_h2plain
(Each row gives an angle x in radians, the CORDIC result, the result of Ghostscript's built-in operator for the same angle \(the built-in sin and cos work in degrees, so x is converted\), and the difference between the two.) sc_para
[ (x) (corsin x) (built-in sin) (difference) (corcos x) (built-in cos) (difference) ]
	/Helvetica-Bold 0.42 sc_tablerow
0.8 sc_rule 3 sc_down
[ -3 -1 -0.5 0 0.3 1 1.5707 2 3 4 6 10 ] {
	/sc_x exch def
	[ sc_x sc_r2s
	  sc_x corsin dup sc_r2s exch sc_x sc_deg sin dup sc_r2s 3 1 roll sub sc_r2s
	  sc_x corcos dup sc_r2s exch sc_x sc_deg cos dup sc_r2s 3 1 roll sub sc_r2s ]
	/Courier 0.12 sc_tablerow
} forall
4 sc_down
(The differences are at the level of single-precision rounding, which is where both CORDIC and the built-in operators run out of digits.) sc_para

% ---- plots
/sc_px { sc_xmin sub sc_xmax sc_xmin sub div sc_W mul sc_L add } def
/sc_py { sc_ymin sub sc_ymax sc_ymin sub div sc_ph mul sc_pb add } def
/sc_N 240 def
/sc_curve { % {f}   stroke f over [sc_xmin, sc_xmax]
	/sc_f exch def newpath
	0 1 sc_N { /sc_i exch def
		sc_i sc_N div sc_xmax sc_xmin sub mul sc_xmin add
		dup sc_px exch sc_f sc_py
		sc_i 0 eq { moveto }{ lineto } ifelse
	} for stroke
} def
/sc_frame { % (ylabel-top) (ylabel-bottom)
	0.85 setgray 0.5 setlinewidth
	sc_L sc_pb sc_W sc_ph rectstroke
	0 sc_px sc_pb moveto 0 sc_ph rlineto
	sc_L 0 sc_py moveto sc_W 0 rlineto stroke
	/Helvetica 7.5 sc_font sc_muted
	sc_L 3 add sc_pb sc_ph add 9 sub moveto exch show
	sc_L 3 add sc_pb 4 add moveto show
	sc_ink
} def
/sc_legend { % (label) {color} y-offset-index
	/sc_li exch def /sc_lc exch def /sc_lt exch def
	/sc_lx sc_R 150 sub def /sc_ly sc_pb sc_ph add 12 sub sc_li 11 mul sub def
	sc_lc 1.6 setlinewidth sc_lx sc_ly 3 add moveto 18 0 rlineto stroke
	/Helvetica 7.5 sc_font sc_ink sc_lx 24 add sc_ly moveto sc_lt show
} def

210 sc_need
(Plot: CORDIC sin and cos over two periods) sc_h2plain
/sc_ph 150 def 170 sc_need
/sc_pb sc_y sc_ph sub 4 sub def
/sc_xmin pi -2 mul def /sc_xmax pi 2 mul def /sc_ymin -1.25 def /sc_ymax 1.9 def
(1.9) (-1.25) sc_frame
0.82 setgray 4 setlinewidth { sc_deg sin } sc_curve { sc_deg cos } sc_curve
1.1 setlinewidth sc_accent { corsin } sc_curve sc_warm { corcos } sc_curve
(built-in sin and cos) { 0.82 setgray } 0 sc_legend
(corsin) { sc_accent } 1 sc_legend
(corcos) { sc_warm } 2 sc_legend
/Helvetica 7.5 sc_font sc_muted
sc_L sc_pb 10 sub moveto (-2 pi) show
sc_R (2 pi) stringwidth pop sub sc_pb 10 sub moveto (2 pi) show
sc_ink /sc_y sc_pb 18 sub def
(The thick gray curves are Ghostscript's built-in sin and cos; the thin colored curves are corsin and corcos drawn on top. They coincide everywhere.) sc_para

170 sc_need
(Plot: CORDIC minus built-in, magnified) sc_h2plain
/sc_emax 0 def
0 1 sc_N { sc_N div sc_xmax sc_xmin sub mul sc_xmin add /sc_x exch def
	/sc_emax sc_emax sc_x corsin sc_x sc_deg sin sub abs sc_max
	                 sc_x corcos sc_x sc_deg cos sub abs sc_max def } for
/sc_ph 110 def 130 sc_need
/sc_pb sc_y sc_ph sub 4 sub def
/sc_ymax sc_emax 2.3 mul def /sc_ymin sc_emax -1.3 mul def
sc_ymax sc_r2s sc_ymin sc_r2s sc_frame
1 setlinewidth
sc_accent { dup corsin exch sc_deg sin sub } sc_curve
sc_warm { dup corcos exch sc_deg cos sub } sc_curve
(corsin - sin) { sc_accent } 0 sc_legend
(corcos - cos) { sc_warm } 1 sc_legend
sc_ink /sc_y sc_pb 18 sub def
(Largest difference over this range: ) sc_emax sc_r2s mergestr
(. The vertical scale is set by that value, so the plot shows the size and pattern of the rounding noise.) mergestr sc_para

% ================================================================ Matrix
(Matrix.ps) sc_h1
(A matrix is stored as an array of columns, and rows and columns are numbered from 1. The results below print a matrix row by row, as [row; row], so [1 2; 3 4] has 1 2 on its first row and 3 4 on its second.) sc_para

(Building and reading) sc_h2
(2 3 marray)
	(Creates a matrix with 2 rows and 3 columns, filled with zeros.) sc_row
(/A 2 2 marray def A 1 1 1 mput A 1 2 2 mput A 2 1 3 mput A 2 2 4 mput A)
	(Builds the 2x2 matrix A = [1 2; 3 4]. mput takes the matrix, a row, a column and a value.) sc_row
(A 2 1 mget)
	(mget reads row 2, column 1 of A: 3.) sc_row
(A msize)
	(msize gives the number of rows, then the number of columns.) sc_row
(A typchk)
	(typchk recognises the array as a matrix.) sc_row

(Building from rows) sc_h2
([[1 2] [3 4]] mfromrows)
	(Builds the same matrix from a list of rows, which is the easiest way to write one down. Rows of different lengths are rejected.) sc_row
(A mtorows 1 get 0 get)
	(mtorows turns a matrix back into a list of rows. Row 2 of A starts with 3.) sc_row
(3 midentity)
	(The 3x3 identity matrix: ones on the diagonal, zeros elsewhere.) sc_row
(A mtranspose)
	(The transpose swaps rows and columns, so the 3 moves from row 2, column 1 to row 1, column 2.) sc_row
(A mcopy dup 1 1 99 mput)
	(mcopy makes an independent copy. Changing the copy leaves A as [1 2; 3 4], which mput on A itself would not.) sc_row

(Arithmetic) sc_h2
(A A madd)
	(Element-by-element sum: each entry of A doubled.) sc_row
(A 2 midentity msub)
	(A minus the identity: 1 is taken from each diagonal entry.) sc_row
(A 10 mscale)
	(Multiplies every element by a number.) sc_row
(A A mmul)
	(Matrix product: [1 2; 3 4] x [1 2; 3 4] = [7 10; 15 22]. Each entry is a row of the first matrix times a column of the second, for example 1x1 + 2x3 = 7.) sc_row
(/B 2 1 marray def B 1 1 5 mput B 2 1 6 mput B)
	(Builds a column vector B = [5; 6], a matrix with 2 rows and 1 column.) sc_row
(A B mmul)
	(A times B: [1x5 + 2x6; 3x5 + 4x6] = [17; 39].) sc_row
(A [[1 2 3]] mfromrows madd)
	(Deliberate error: a 2x2 matrix and a 1x3 matrix can't be added, so madd raises rangecheck.) sc_row
(2 3 marray 2 2 marray mmul)
	(Deliberate mismatch: a 2x3 matrix can't multiply a 2x2 one, because its 3 columns must meet 3 rows. For compatibility with the 2012 version, mmul returns the string Nan here instead of raising an error.) sc_row

(Determinant, inverse and solving) sc_h2
(A mdet)
	(The determinant: 1x4 - 2x3 = -2. A matrix of integers always has an integer determinant, so mdet rounds the result to one.) sc_row
(A minv)
	(The inverse, found by Gauss-Jordan elimination with row swaps. With ordinary reals each entry is accurate to about 7 digits; they print rounded to 6.) sc_row
(A A minv mmul)
	(A times its inverse gives the identity, up to the rounding of ordinary reals.) sc_row
(A B msolve)
	(Solves A x = B without forming the inverse: x = [-4; 4.5]. Check: 1x\(-4\) + 2x4.5 = 5 and 3x\(-4\) + 4x4.5 = 6.) sc_row
([[1 2 3] [4 5 6] [7 8 9]] mfromrows mdet)
	(This matrix is singular: its third row is twice the second minus the first, so the determinant is 0.) sc_row
([[1 2 3] [4 5 6] [7 8 9]] mfromrows minv)
	(Deliberate error: a singular matrix has no inverse, so minv raises undefinedresult. msolve does the same.) sc_row

110 sc_need (Exact decimals with bignums) sc_h2plain
(If any element is a bignum or a string such as \(0.1\), the whole calculation uses bignum arithmetic at the current precision. Strings are read with setbn, so they are exact decimals. These rows use 12 digits to keep them short.) sc_para
sc_labels
([[0.1 0.2] [0.3 0.4]] mfromrows mdet 0.02 add)
	(With ordinary reals, 0.1 to 0.4 are stored as binary fractions. The determinant prints as -0.02, but adding 0.02 back leaves about -4e-9 instead of 0.) sc_row
(12 setbnsize [[\(0.1\) \(0.2\)] [\(0.3\) \(0.4\)]] mfromrows mdet)
	(The same matrix written with strings: the determinant is exactly -0.02.) sc_row
([[\(0.1\) \(0.2\)] [\(0.3\) \(0.4\)]] mfromrows minv)
	(Its inverse is exactly [-20 10; 15 -5].) sc_row
([ 1 1 3 { /i exch def [ 1 1 3 { /j exch def 1 i j add 1 sub div } for ] } for ] mfromrows minv)
	(The 3x3 Hilbert matrix, with entries 1/\(i+j-1\), is a classic hard case: small rounding errors grow large in its inverse. The exact inverse is [9 -36 30; -36 192 -180; 30 -180 180], and ordinary reals get only about 5 digits right.) sc_row
([ 1 1 3 { /i exch def [ 1 1 3 { /j exch def 1 setbn i j add 1 sub setbn divbn } for ] } for ] mfromrows minv)
	(The same matrix with 12-digit bignums. Its entries 1/3 and 1/5 are rounded to 12 digits, so the inverse is not exact either, but about 10 of its 12 digits are right. More digits give a closer answer.) sc_row
20 setbnsize

(Least squares and eigenvalues) sc_h2
([[1 1] [1 2] [1 3] [1 4]] mfromrows [[6] [5] [7] [10]] mfromrows mlstsq)
	(The best straight line y = a + bx through \(1, 6\), \(2, 5\), \(3, 7\), \(4, 10\): a = 3.5, b = 1.4. mlstsq solves an overdetermined system by Householder QR, which is more accurate than the normal equations.) sc_row
([[1 2] [2 4] [3 6]] mfromrows [[1] [2] [3]] mfromrows mlstsq)
	(Deliberate error: the second column is twice the first, so there is no unique answer and mlstsq raises undefinedresult.) sc_row
([[2 1] [1 2]] mfromrows meigsym pop)
	(meigsym returns the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix in ascending order, here 1 and 3, found by cyclic Jacobi rotations. This row drops the second result to show the first.) sc_rowl
([[2 1] [1 2]] mfromrows meigsym exch pop)
	(The second result holds the unit eigenvectors as columns: [0.707; -0.707] for 1 and [0.707; 0.707] for 3.) sc_row
([[4 1 2] [1 3 0] [2 0 5]] mfromrows meigsym pop)
	(The three eigenvalues of a 3x3 symmetric matrix. They add up to 12, the sum of the diagonal.) sc_rowl
([[\(4\) 1 2] [1 3 0] [2 0 5]] mfromrows meigsym pop)
	(The same eigenvalues with 20-digit bignums.) sc_rowl
([[1 2] [3 4]] mfromrows meigsym)
	(Deliberate error: meigsym only accepts symmetric matrices, and raises rangecheck otherwise.) sc_row

% ================================================================ Complex
(Complex.ps) sc_h1
(A complex number is the array [re im], and results print as re + im i. Wherever a complex number is expected, a plain number x counts as [x 0]. As with matrices, if any part is a bignum or a string such as \(0.1\), the calculation uses bignums.) sc_para

(Arithmetic) sc_h2
([1 2] [3 4] cadd)
	(\(1 + 2i\) + \(3 + 4i\).) sc_row
([1 2] [3 4] cmul)
	(\(1 + 2i\)\(3 + 4i\) = 3 + 4i + 6i + 8i^2 = -5 + 10i, since i^2 = -1.) sc_row
([1 2] [3 4] cdiv)
	(Division multiplies top and bottom by the conjugate 3 - 4i: \(11 + 2i\)/25.) sc_row
([1 2] cconj)
	(The conjugate flips the sign of the imaginary part. cneg flips both.) sc_row
([3 4] cabs)
	(The modulus, the distance from 0: sqrt\(3^2 + 4^2\) = 5.) sc_row
([0 1] carg)
	(The argument, the angle from the positive real axis, in radians: i is at pi/2.) sc_row
([1 2] [0 0] cdiv)
	(Deliberate error: division by zero raises undefinedresult.) sc_row

(Functions) sc_h2
([-4 0] csqrt)
	(The square root of -4 is 2i. csqrt gives the root with a positive real part.) sc_row
([0 1] 2 cpow)
	(i squared is -1. Whole-number powers use repeated multiplication.) sc_row
([\(1\) 2] 10 cpow)
	(\(1 + 2i\)^10 with bignums, exact: 237 - 3116i.) sc_row
([\(0\) 1] [0 1] cpow)
	(i to the power i is a real number, e^\(-pi/2\) = 0.2079. General powers use e^\(w ln z\).) sc_row
([0 pibn] cexp)
	(e^\(i pi\) = -1. The tiny imaginary part is not an error in cexp: pibn gives pi rounded to 20 digits, which is slightly too large, and the sine of that is about -3.7e-20.) sc_row
([0 1] cln)
	(The principal logarithm: ln i = i pi/2.) sc_row
([0 0] cln)
	(Deliberate error: the logarithm of 0 is undefined.) sc_row

% ================================================================ Numeric
(Numeric.ps) sc_h1
(Numerical methods for ordinary reals or bignums. A function is passed as a procedure that takes x and returns f\(x\); with bignums it receives a bignum and must return one, so it uses addbn, mulbn and the rest.) sc_para

(Whole numbers) sc_h2
(84 36 gcd)
	(The greatest common divisor of 84 and 36, by Euclid's algorithm.) sc_row
(84 36 lcm)
	(The least common multiple.) sc_row

(Roots of a function) sc_h2
({ dup mul 2 sub } 0 2 froot)
	(The x between 0 and 2 where x^2 - 2 = 0. froot needs a range where f changes sign, and narrows it with the Illinois method.) sc_row
({ dup mulbn 2 setbn subbn } \(1\) \(2\) froot)
	(The same root with bignums: the square root of 2 to 20 digits.) sc_row
({ cosbn } \(1\) \(2\) froot)
	(Where cos x = 0 between 1 and 2: pi/2.) sc_row
({ dup mul 1 add } 0 2 froot)
	(Deliberate error: x^2 + 1 has no sign change \(no real root\), so froot raises rangecheck.) sc_row

(Integrals) sc_h2
({ dup mul } 0 1 fintegrate)
	(The area under x^2 from 0 to 1, which is 1/3. fintegrate uses Gauss-Legendre rules with 16 and 32 points, and halves the interval wherever they disagree.) sc_row
({ sqrt } 0 1 fintegrate)
	(The square root has an infinite slope at 0, which polynomial-based rules handle badly, so the interval is halved repeatedly near 0. The answer, 2/3, is still right to the precision of a real.) sc_row
(12 setbnsize { 1 setbn exch dup mulbn 1 setbn addbn divbn } \(0\) \(1\) fintegrate)
	(The integral of 1/\(1 + x^2\) from 0 to 1 is pi/4, here to 12 digits. The first bignum integral at a precision is slow, about 10 seconds, while the rule's points and weights are worked out; they are kept for later calls.) sc_row
(20 setbnsize)
	(Back to 20 digits.) sc_row

(Polynomials) sc_h2
(A polynomial is a list of coefficients, highest power first: [1 0 -2] is x^2 - 2.) sc_para
sc_labels
([1 0 -2] 3 polyval)
	(x^2 - 2 at x = 3: 7. polyval uses Horner's rule.) sc_row
([1 0 -2] [0 1] polyval)
	(At x = i the value is i^2 - 2 = -3.) sc_row
([1 2 3] polyder)
	(The derivative of x^2 + 2x + 3 is 2x + 2.) sc_rowl
([1 2] [1 -2] polymul)
	(\(x + 2\)\(x - 2\) = x^2 - 4. polyadd adds two polynomials.) sc_rowl
([1 2 5] polyroots)
	(All roots, complex included, sorted by real part: x^2 + 2x + 5 has roots -1 - 2i and -1 + 2i. polyroots uses the Durand-Kerner iteration.) sc_rowl
([\(1\) -6 11 -6] polyroots)
	(The roots of x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6 = \(x-1\)\(x-2\)\(x-3\), to 20 digits.) sc_rowl
([1 -2 1] polyroots)
	(\(x - 1\)^2 has 1 as a double root. Repeated roots converge slowly and keep only about half the digits, which shows here as small errors.) sc_rowl
([0 1 2 3 4] [1.1 1.9 5.2 9.8 17.1] 2 polyfit)
	(The least-squares parabola through five points, as coefficients of x^2, x and 1. polyfit builds the system and solves it with mlstsq.) sc_rowl
([0 1 2 3 4] [\(1.1\) \(1.9\) \(5.2\) \(9.8\) \(17.1\)] 2 polyfit)
	(The same fit with bignums. The exact coefficients are 143/140, -67/700 and 379/350, repeating decimals that are rounded to 20 digits.) sc_rowl

% ================================================================ other
(Utilities) sc_h1
(Helpers) sc_h2
(\(ab\) \(cd\) mergestr)
	(mergestr joins two strings into a new one.) sc_row
(1 setbn typchk)
	(typchk names the library's own types: here a bignum,) sc_row
([1 2] typchk)
	(a complex number,) sc_row
(A typchk)
	(a matrix,) sc_row
(5 typchk)
	(and for anything else, PostScript's own type name: an integer,) sc_row
(\(text\) typchk)
	(or a string.) sc_row
(bnDebug)
	(The debug switch, off by default. Set it to true to make setbn and the arithmetic procedures print each result as they go.) sc_row

sc_footer showpage
%%EOF
